diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1eda38a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,189 @@
+## Chat bots
+
+Bots are `User` records with the `bot` role (`User::Role`). Everything else, like memberships,
+avatars, message authorship, broadcasts, and push notifications, is the same as for people. One
+exception: bots don't join open rooms automatically unless "Add to all open rooms" is on (see
+below). They can be added to closed rooms or direct rooms like anyone else.
+
+### Room membership ("Add to all open rooms")
+
+- `users.joins_all_open_rooms` (boolean, default `false`) is the "Add to all open rooms" switch on the
+  bot form (a real checkbox styled as `.switch`, permitted in `BotsController#bot_params`).
+- `User#member_of_all_open_rooms?` / `User.members_of_all_open_rooms` = people, plus bots with the
+  flag. That scope decides who is granted membership when a room becomes open
+  (`Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users`), and `User`'s `after_create_commit
+  :grant_membership_to_open_rooms` only runs for those users.
+- Turning the flag on (`User::Bot` `after_update_commit :join_all_open_rooms`) joins every existing
+  open room. Turning it off removes nothing.
+- Don't reuse `grant_membership_to_open_rooms` as the name of the update callback: `after_create_commit`
+  and `after_update_commit` with the same method name override each other.
+- The open room form (`Rooms::OpensController#new/edit`) lists `members_of_all_open_rooms` plus
+  current members, so it doesn't show bots that aren't in the room.
+- There's no UI yet for a person to add a flag-less bot to an *open* room (open rooms have no member
+  picker); closed rooms use the usual member switches.
+
+### Data
+
+- `users.bot_token`: a random 24-character alphanumeric secret (unique index). It's `nil` for
+  people and for deleted bots.
+- `users.creator_id`: the person who created the bot. `User#bots` lists the bots someone owns.
+  There's no DB-level foreign key, because `User.destroy_all` (used in tests) would trip over the
+  self-reference. Instead, `has_many :bots, dependent: :nullify` handles it.
+- `users.password_digest` is nullable, because bots have no password or email, so they can't sign in.
+
+### Bot code (bot key)
+
+The "Bot code" shown in the UI is `User#bot_key`, which is `"<id>-<bot_token>"`.
+`User.authenticate_bot(key)` looks the bot up by id among *active* bots and compares the token
+with `secure_compare`. Banned or deactivated bots, and people, never authenticate.
+
+The bot code is a credential:
+- Only the creator and administrators can see it: `BotsController#manageable_bots` scopes every
+  action, and anyone else gets a 404.
+- The bots pages are sent with `Cache-Control: no-store` and `turbo-cache-control: no-cache`.
+- `bot_key` is filtered from logged params (it matches `_key` in `filter_parameters`), and
+  `lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb` masks the key in the request path, which Rails logs
+  in its "Started POST …" line.
+
+### Posting API
+
+`POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages` → `Messages::ByBotsController#create`
+(route helper: `room_bot_messages_url(room, bot.bot_key)`).
+
+- `Authentication.allow_bot_access` skips session auth and CSRF protection, then sets
+  `Current.user` to the authenticated bot. If the key is invalid, it returns `401`.
+- If the room isn't one of the bot's rooms (or doesn't exist), it returns `404`.
+- The raw request body (`request.raw_post`) becomes the message body. This works with
+  `curl -d`, even though curl sends it form-encoded. For multipart requests, the `attachment`
+  field (plus an optional `body` field) is used instead.
+- An empty body with no attachment returns `422`. On success it returns `201` with
+  `Location: /messages/:id`.
+- It reuses `create_with_attachment!` and `broadcast_create`, and `Message`'s
+  `after_create_commit` → `Room#receive` (unread state and web push), so bot messages behave
+  exactly like human ones.
+
+### UI
+
+- `/account/edit` has a nav link (bot icon), "Set up chat bots", that goes to `/bots`.
+- `/bots` (`BotsController`) has index/new/create/edit/update/destroy and shows every bot on
+  one page. "Delete" deactivates the bot through `User#deactivate`: memberships are removed,
+  `bot_token` is cleared, and the bot's past messages stay.
+- Bots are left out of people-oriented lists: the account users list (`AccountsController`,
+  `Accounts::UsersController`) and the sidebar's direct-message placeholders.
+
+### Webhooks (bots that answer)
+
+A bot may have one `Webhook` (`webhooks` table: `user_id` unique, `url`). The bot form edits it
+through the virtual `User#webhook_url` attribute (`has_one :webhook, autosave: true`): a URL
+creates or updates the webhook, and a blank one marks it for destruction when the bot is saved. Only
+`http(s)` URLs are valid. Error messages say "Webhook URL" (`config/locales/en.yml`, key
+`user/webhook.url`). Deactivating a bot deletes its webhook.
+
+**Trigger:** `Message` `after_create_commit` → `Room#receive` → `deliver_webhooks_later` →
+`Message#mentioned_bot_webhooks` → `Webhook#deliver_later` → `Webhook::DeliveryJob`. That's one
+job per mentioned bot, so the person posting never waits on a bot. (In development the Active
+Job adapter is `:async`, in production it's Resque.)
+- Only mentions trigger a webhook: active bots that are members of the room, have a webhook, and
+  aren't the author.
+- Messages *created by bots* never trigger webhooks. That prevents bot↔bot reply loops, and it means
+  webhook replies and API posts don't call other bots.
+- Edits don't trigger webhooks either.
+
+**Payload** (`Webhook#payload_for`, POSTed as `Content-Type: application/json`):
+`event` (`"mention"`), `author {id,name}`,
+`room {id,name,reply_to}` (where `reply_to` = `room_bot_messages_path(room, bot.bot_key)`), and
+`message {id, body {text, html}, link}` (where `link` = `room_at_message_path`, i.e.
+`/rooms/:id/@:message_id`). Paths are relative.
+- `body.html` is the stored rich-text HTML (`message.body.body.to_html`), including
+  `<action-text-attachment>` mention tags.
+- `body.text` is plain text with *this bot's* mention attachments removed (other mentions are kept as
+  `@Name`), blanks squeezed, and the ends stripped (including NBSPs).
+
+**Reply** (`Webhook#reply_to`): a `200` with `text/plain` (HTML-escaped, newlines become `<br>`)
+or `text/html` (stored as-is, and Action Text sanitizes it when rendering) is posted as the bot via
+`create_with_attachment!` + `broadcast_create`. Anything else posts nothing: `204`, other statuses,
+other content types, empty bodies, or bodies over 1 MB. Redirects aren't followed. Open/read/write
+timeouts are 7s (`ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT`). Network errors and timeouts are logged and swallowed, with no
+retry, so replies can't be duplicated. Before delivering, the job checks that the bot is still
+active and still in the room.
+
+**`joined` event:** whenever a bot is added to a non-direct room, its webhook gets
+`{ event: "joined", room: {id,name,reply_to} }` (`Webhook#joined_payload_for`, same `room` hash as
+mentions), via `Webhook#deliver_joined_later` → `Webhook::JoinedDeliveryJob` → `Webhook#deliver_joined`.
+The reply is handled exactly like a mention reply.
+- Every membership grant goes through `Membership.grant(attributes)` (`Membership::BotGreeting`), used by
+  `room.memberships.grant_to` and `User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms`. It `insert_all`s with
+  `returning: room_id, user_id`. Existing memberships are skipped by the unique index and not returned,
+  so a bot hears about each room once per actual addition (re-adding after removal sends it again).
+  **New code that creates memberships must go through `Membership.grant`**, not `Membership.insert_all`.
+- Jobs are enqueued in `ActiveRecord.after_all_transactions_commit`, because
+  `enqueue_after_transaction_commit` is off and rooms/memberships are often created in a transaction
+  (`Room.create_for`, `memberships.revise`).
+- Only active bots with a webhook get it. Direct rooms are filtered out both at enqueue time and in
+  `deliver_joined`. The job also checks that the bot is still active and in the room.
+
+**Not SSRF-guarded on purpose:** unlike `Opengraph::Fetch`, webhook URLs may point at private or
+loopback addresses, since bots are commonly self-hosted next to Campfire (and QA uses a local
+server). If that ever changes, `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard` is the tool to use.
+
+### Bots per member (limit)
+
+- `Account#settings.bots_per_member` (`has_json` integer, default `5`). It's validated as a whole
+  number ≥ 0 (`Account#bots_per_member_is_a_whole_number`). `AccountsController#update` redirects
+  back with a flash alert when it's invalid instead of raising. Administrators edit it on
+  `/account/edit` ("Bots per member", a number field that submits on change; `0` means members
+  can't create bots at all).
+- `User#can_create_bots?` (in `User::Bot`): administrators are always allowed. Everyone else needs
+  `bots.not_deactivated.count < bots_per_member`, so deleted (deactivated) bots don't count, but banned ones do.
+- Enforcement:
+  - `/bots` hides "Add a chat bot" and shows "You've reached the limit of N bots per member"
+    (`#bot_limit_reached`).
+  - `BotsController#ensure_can_create_bots` (`new`/`create`) redirects to `/bots` with an alert.
+  - A `User` create validation (`within_creators_bot_limit`, only for bots with a creator) backs
+    this up for any other code path that calls `User.create_bot!`.
+- Lowering the limit never touches existing bots: the check only runs when a bot is created, so
+  existing bots can still be edited and posted with.
+
+### Misbehaving bots (flood control, failing webhooks, suspension)
+
+The limits are constants in code; there are no settings for them.
+
+**Flooding** (`User::FloodControl`): a bot may post at most `POSTING_LIMIT` (10) messages in any
+`POSTING_PERIOD` (10s). The count is simply the bot's messages with `created_at` in the window, so API
+posts and webhook replies both count, and rejected attempts don't. Every post goes through
+`bot.post_without_flooding { create the message }`:
+- The check runs inside `with_lock` on the bot row, but **the message is created outside that
+  transaction**. Attachments are uploaded on commit and `Message::Attachment` analyzes them right
+  away, so creating the message inside the lock raises `ActiveStorage::FileNotFoundError`.
+- The post that goes over the limit is rejected and sets `users.posting_cooldown_ends_at` to 30s
+  from now (`COOLDOWN`), with `posting_attempts_during_cooldown` reset to 0. Rejections raise
+  `User::FloodControl::Flooding` (`#retry_after` = seconds left, rounded up, at least 1).
+- Each attempt during the cooldown (API or webhook reply) increments
+  `posting_attempts_during_cooldown`. The 5th one (`ATTEMPTS_DURING_COOLDOWN_BEFORE_SUSPENSION`)
+  suspends the bot with reason `flooding`. That attempt itself still gets a `429`, and later ones get `403`.
+- `Messages::ByBotsController` turns `Flooding` into `429` plus `Retry-After`. `Webhook#reply_to`
+  logs it and posts nothing. An empty body still gets `422`, and isn't counted as an attempt.
+
+**Failing webhooks** (`Webhook#record_delivery`): `webhooks.consecutive_failures` is incremented
+atomically (`update_counters`) for any non-2xx response (redirects included), timeout or network
+error, and reset to 0 by any 2xx (even a `204` with no reply). At `FAILURES_BEFORE_SUSPENSION` (10)
+the bot is suspended with reason `failing_webhook`. Deliveries skipped because the bot isn't
+deliverable aren't counted.
+
+**Suspension** (`User::Suspendable`): `users.suspended_at` and `users.suspension_reason`
+(`"flooding"` / `"failing_webhook"`, described by `SUSPENSION_REASONS` /
+`#suspension_reason_description`). The bot's `status` stays `active` on purpose:
+- its bot code still authenticates, so the posting API can tell it apart from a bad key (`403`
+  from `ensure_bot_is_not_suspended`, not `401`);
+- it keeps its memberships and messages, and still counts towards `can_create_bots?` (which counts
+  `not_deactivated`);
+- it's still in `User.active.bot`, so it stays listed on `/bots`, with "Suspended" and the reason
+  (`#bot_suspension_<id>` in `bots/_bot`).
+- It gets no webhooks: it's filtered out with `not_suspended` at enqueue time
+  (`Message#mentioned_bot_webhooks`, `Membership::BotGreeting`) and again in `Webhook#deliverable_to?`.
+  **New code that delivers to bots must check `suspended?` too.**
+
+**Reactivation**: `POST /bots/:bot_id/reactivation` (`Bots::ReactivationsController`,
+`ensure_can_administer`, so administrators only; even the bot's creator gets a `403`). On `/bots`,
+administrators see a "Reactivate" button (`button_to` + `turbo_confirm`, i.e. the native `confirm()`).
+`User#reactivate` clears the suspension and the cooldown, and resets the webhook's failure count.
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d3def4f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Bot keys travel in the URL path (/rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages), which parameter filtering
+# doesn't cover. Mask them in the filtered path so they don't end up in request logs.
+module FilteredBotKeyPath
+  BOT_KEY_PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/?]+(/messages/?)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def filtered_path
+    @filtered_bot_key_path ||= super.sub(BOT_KEY_PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend FilteredBotKeyPath
